\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Which exogenous series the models use. Each series' share of the exogenous attribution, pooled over the 126{} monthly origins: the tree ensembles' share of the mean absolute SHAP value (filled markers) and the ridge's share of the absolute standardized coefficients $|\gamma |$ (open diamonds). Rows: the 20 series with the largest mean share over the three tree ensembles, in that order, then (below the dotted line) the two series in the ridge's top ten that are not among them. Non-standard series are labeled in bold. The two measures differ; the figure compares rankings and shares, not magnitudes (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref {tab:rh_fip_shares}).}}{29}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rh_fip_rank}{{1}{29}{Which exogenous series the models use. Each series' share of the exogenous attribution, pooled over the \rhfipNOrigins {} monthly origins: the tree ensembles' share of the mean absolute SHAP value (filled markers) and the ridge's share of the absolute standardized coefficients $|\gamma |$ (open diamonds). Rows: the 20 series with the largest mean share over the three tree ensembles, in that order, then (below the dotted line) the two series in the ridge's top ten that are not among them. Non-standard series are labeled in bold. The two measures differ; the figure compares rankings and shares, not magnitudes (Table~\ref {tab:rh_fip_shares})}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Variable importance over time. (a) The 36{} non-standard series' share of each model's exogenous attribution by calendar year: tree ensembles (solid) by the year of the forecast bar, linear models (dashed) by the year of the origin whose coefficients enter. (b), (c) The share of the exogenous total by year for the leading series of LightGBM and of the ridge (the union of each model's eight largest pooled shares; non-standard series in bold), on one square-root color scale. Shares of each model's own measure: mean absolute SHAP value for the trees, $|\gamma |$ for the linear models. The linear shares by year are computed here from the coefficients at the origins (descriptive). 2024{} covers January to April.}}{30}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rh_fip_time}{{2}{30}{Variable importance over time. (a) The \rhfipNNonStd {} non-standard series' share of each model's exogenous attribution by calendar year: tree ensembles (solid) by the year of the forecast bar, linear models (dashed) by the year of the origin whose coefficients enter. (b), (c) The share of the exogenous total by year for the leading series of LightGBM and of the ridge (the union of each model's eight largest pooled shares; non-standard series in bold), on one square-root color scale. Shares of each model's own measure: mean absolute SHAP value for the trees, $|\gamma |$ for the linear models. The linear shares by year are computed here from the coefficients at the origins (descriptive). \rhfipYearLast {} covers January to April}{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces When the tree ensembles use the non-standard series. Share of the non-standard series in the total mean absolute SHAP value on subsets of the 33{,}970{} bars, with 95\% block-bootstrap intervals over the origins. (a) By bar of the day (top; bands: intervals) and the FOMC-announcement category's share (bottom). (b) On the 78{} scheduled FOMC statement days and on all other days, and by the tercile of the VIX close before the origin (cut points 13.96{} and 19.02; every bar of an origin's block takes that origin's tercile).}}{31}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rh_fip_cond}{{3}{31}{When the tree ensembles use the non-standard series. Share of the non-standard series in the total mean absolute SHAP value on subsets of the \rhfipNRows {} bars, with \rhfipCiLevel \% block-bootstrap intervals over the origins. (a) By bar of the day (top; bands: intervals) and the FOMC-announcement category's share (bottom). (b) On the \rhfipFomcDays {} scheduled FOMC statement days and on all other days, and by the tercile of the VIX close before the origin (cut points \rhfipVixLo {} and \rhfipVixHi ; every bar of an origin's block takes that origin's tercile)}{figure.caption.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Response shapes of the leading non-standard series. For each series, the mean SHAP value of the series (all its predictors) in each decile of its dominant predictor, the value average at horizon $k$ with the largest mean absolute SHAP value, as a percentage of the mean forecast; over all 33{,}970{} bars. The predictor values are the robust-scaled inputs the trees see. A model's line is drawn where the series' share is at least 0.5\% (the classification in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref {tab:rh_fip_nonstd}); the five series with the largest mean share over the three tree ensembles and the next row of that table.}}{32}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rh_fip_shapes}{{4}{32}{Response shapes of the leading non-standard series. For each series, the mean SHAP value of the series (all its predictors) in each decile of its dominant predictor, the value average at horizon $k$ with the largest mean absolute SHAP value, as a percentage of the mean forecast; over all \rhfipNRows {} bars. The predictor values are the robust-scaled inputs the trees see. A model's line is drawn where the series' share is at least \rhfipShapeMin \% (the classification in Table~\ref {tab:rh_fip_nonstd}); the five series with the largest mean share over the three tree ensembles and the next row of that table}{figure.caption.14}{}}
